InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
Prerequisite
Before starting Instrumentation - 2, it’s an absolute necessity that you understand stuffs form Instrumen-
tation - 1.  You can find it here.  Also, one need to have a fundamental grasp of positional astronomy and
coordinate systems.  You can follow AO Guide - Positional Astronomy.  If you don’t have a telescope to
play around with, watch videos on the internet to familiarize yourself.
1  Telescope Mounts
A telescope mount is the mechanical platform that holds and moves the optical tube assembly.  Its primary
purpose is to provide a stable, movable support structure that allows the telescope to be pointed at
celestial targets.
Mounts are classified by their axes of rotation and tracking method.  The two fundamental types are
the altazimuth mount, which moves in altitude (up/down) and azimuth (left/right), and the equatorial
mount, which aligns one axis with Earth’s rotational axis (Right ascension α and Declination δ).
Olympiad relevancy:
In the IOAA and the IAO, the telescope round often tests mount operation skills.  Different mounts
appear each year:  a German equatorial mount was used in IOAA 2025 and IAO 2023, a Galileoscope
with its simple altazimuth mount in IOAA 2024, and a Dobsonian mount in IOAA 2023.  We’ll mostly
talk about an Alt-az, a German equatorial, and a Dobsonian.  There are some other mounts, like a Fork
mount or Horseshoe mount, but they aren’t often seen in IOAA.
1.1  Basic Altazimuth Mount
The altazimuth mount is a two-axis mount that allows movement in altitude (vertical angle) and azimuth
(horizontal angle).  Its name is derived from ”altitude” and ”azimuth.” The altitude axis (an imaginary
line about which a body rotates) is horizontal and allows the telescope to tilt up and down.  The azimuth
axis is vertical and allows the telescope to rotate left and right parallel to the ground plane.  This design
mimics the natural motion of looking around the sky:  up/down and left/right.
A basic altazimuth mount consists of a base that rotates on a bearing around the vertical azimuth axis.
Mounted on this base is a cradle or fork that holds the telescope tube and pivots around the horizontal
altitude axis.  The altitude axis must be precisely perpendicular to the azimuth axis to avoid pointing
3

InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
errors.  Friction, bearing quality, and balance determine the smoothness of motion.
To track an object automatically, an altazimuth mount requires two independent motors,one on each
axis,driven at continuously variable, non-constant rates.  The required angular velocities
 ̇
A
(azimuth rate)
and   ̇a (altitude rate) for an object at azimuth A, altitude a, latitude φ, and sidereal rate ω are given:
 ̇
A = ω(sinφ + cosφ cosA tana)
 ̇a = ω cosφ sinA
1.1.1    Manual Handling
The manual movement is typically controlled through manual slow-motion controls and free-hand motion.
•  Free-hand movement
Free-hand movement refers to directly grasping the telescope tube or a handle on the mount and
physically moving it in altitude and azimuth.  This is common on simpler mounts, especially Dobsonians
and basic tripod-mounted refractors.  The telescope tube is typically held in place (locked at one exact
alt-azimuth) by adjustable friction clutches or locking knobs.  When these clutches are loosened, the
telescope can be moved smoothly and freely in any direction.  This freehand movement is used for
initial rough pointing toward a target.
•  Fine-adjustment cables
Once the target is approximately centered in the finderscope or eyepiece, the observer tightens the
clutches just enough to allow fine adjustment.  Most manual altazimuth mounts are equipped with
slow-motion control knobs or fine-adjustment cables.  These are connected to worm gears or tangent
arms that provide small angular movements without disturbing the telescope’s balance.
The altitude slow-motion knob moves the telescope up and down, while the azimuth slow-motion knob
rotates it left and right.  You can use these knobs to move the telescope at a controlled, slow rate, for
accurate centering of objects in the field of view.  These controls are necessary for tracking objects
manually during observation, and the observer must continuously adjust both axes to compensate for
Earth’s rotation.
Above is how a fire-adjustment cable looks (enclosed in a yellow shape, kinda contrasts with the white
background, but it’s fine).  In observational tasks during astronomy olympiads, participants may be
required to use these knobs to point at the celestial targets.  It’s better to have hands-on experience with
the telescope.
