1.1.2    Field Rotation:  Avoid Alt-az Mount for Astrophotography
This content is from StarMaps101 by Fahim Rajit Hossein.
Alt-az mounts are to be avoided due to a phenomenon known as field rotation.  Field rotation occurs
due to the design of the alt-az mount.  One axis, the azimuth axis, rotates around a vertical line passing
from the center of the telescope to the zenith (left to right).  The altitude axis rotates about a horizontal
axis (up and down).  Both axes must rotate at different rates to maintain the object in the field of view
(FOV). As the Earth rotates about its axis, the central object in the field of view will remain centered,
but other objects in the FOV will appear to rotate with elapsed time.  The orientation of the sky in
your FOV slowly twists, and overtime star forms arcs instead of points, which eventually ruins long
exposure.  Field Rotation is an undesirable effect on time-exposure astrophotographs caused when an
“Alt-Az” (“altitude-azimuth” or alt-azimuth) mount is used to hold and point a telescope with a camera.
It is also sometimes called Frame Rotation because the objects framed by the photograph seem to have
rotated while the picture was taken.  Check this video here
Let the FOV of the telescope be represented by a square and follow the motion of Sagittarius as it rises
in the east.  Because the alt-az mount can only move around the azimuth axis and up and down on
the altitude axis, the FOV remains fixed with the bottom parallel to the horizon.  The objects in the
sky, however, are rotating around the celestial pole star, Polaris.  So asterisms and binary stars appear
to rotate in the FOV. An equatorial mounted telescope does not suffer from field rotation because the
5

InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
telescope is polar aligned, and the FOV does not remain horizontal as it tracks; the telescope rotates.  So
the FOV matches the rotation of the object, and it remains in the same fixed position.
Mathematics behind Field Rotation:
Stars appear to rise in the east and set in the west, rotating about the north celestial pole star, Polaris.
Rising stars will reach their highest elevation in the sky as they cross the local meridian, due south of the
line between Polaris and the zenith.  The altitude indicates the vertical position of the star with 90
◦
at
the horizon and 90
◦
at the zenith.  From direct observation, the following aspects are known about the
rate of rotation:
1.  The maximum rate of field rotation occurs as the star passes through the zenith.
2.  The rate of field rotation is minimal when a star passes through the prime vertical at the horizon.
3.
Considering the azimuth only, the rate of field rotation is highest when the star passes through 0
◦
(N)
and 180
◦
(S), and lowest when the star passes through 90
◦
(E) and 270
◦
(W).
4.
Regarding latitude, field rotation is highest at the Earth’s equator and non-existent at the NCP and
SCP.
The field rotation rate
 ̇
θ(in degrees per second) for an observer at latitudeφ, tracking an object at
altitude a and azimuth A, with sidereal angular velocity ω, is given by:
 ̇
θ =
ω· cosφ· cosA
cosa
where,ω= 4.178×10
−3
degrees/second; Earth’s rotation.  For small altitudes,cosa≈1, and the rotation
is approximately constant.  Near the zenith (a→90
◦
),cosa→0, making the rotation rate theoretically
infinite,this is the ”zenith blind spot” where altazimuth tracking fails.
1.2  Basic Equatorial Mount (EQ)
The equatorial mount is a telescope mounting system designed with one rotational axis aligned parallel to
Earth’s rotational axis.  This axis is called the polar axis or right ascension axis.  The second axis, called
the declination axis, is perpendicular to the polar axis.  When properly aligned, the polar axis points
toward the celestial pole.  Check this YouTube video here.
Why EQ mount over Altazimuth Mount?
Equatorial mounts are preferred over alt-az mounts because they require motion in only one axis to
6

InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
track (during long exposure astrophotography.  It follows the rotation of sky, so its easier to track the
object.  They also eliminate field rotation during long-exposure astrophotography.  Additionally, their
axes align directly with celestial coordinates, which makes object location and navigation more easier for
observational astronomy.
1.2.1    Components in EQ mount
1.  Polar or Right Ascension (α) axis:
The Right Ascension (RA) axis is the primary rotational axis of an equatorial mount.  It is aligned
parallel to Earth’s rotational axis, pointing toward the celestial pole.  When properly aligned, this axis
remains fixed relative to the stars while Earth rotates beneath it.
The RA axis has sidereal tracking.  During astrophotography, by rotating the telescope around this
axis at exactly 15.041
′′
per second (in a motorized system) (one complete revolution in 23 hours 56
minutes 4 seconds), the mount compensates for Earth’s rotation.  This constant tracking rate applies
to all celestial objects regardless of their position in the sky.  In equatorial coordinates, rotation around
the RA axis changes only the right ascension coordinate of the telescope’s pointing direction.  The
declination remains constant during pure RA motion.
2.  Declination (δ) axis:
The Declination (Dec) axis is perpendicular to the RA axis.  Rotation around this axis changes only
the declination coordinate of the telescope’s pointing direction, while right ascension remains constant
during pure declination motion.
7

InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
During astrophotography, the declination axis is fixed relative to the RA axis.  This means that once
an object is centered in the field of view, only the RA axis needs to move to track it.  The declination
axis remains stationary during tracking, except when making corrections for polar alignment errors or
guiding.
3.  Setting Circles (Dial)
Equatorial mounts often feature graduated dials called setting circles on both the Right Ascension
(RA) and Declination axes.  These circles allow the observer to set or read the telescope’s pointing
coordinates directly without electronic aids.
(a)  Right Ascension Setting Circle
The RA setting circle is mounted on the polar (RA) axis.  It is typically marked in hours, minutes,
and sometimes seconds, covering the full 24-hour range of right ascension from 0h to 23h 59m 59s.
However, since sidereal time advances at a rate slightly different from solar time, the RA circle
corresponds to sidereal hours.  To use the RA circle effectively, the observer must first synchronize
it with the current sidereal time or the RA of a known star.  The procedure is:
1.  Polar align your telescope.  (We’ll deal with it in the next subsection)
2.  Point the telescope at a star of known right ascension.
3.  Rotate the RA setting circle until it reads the star’s RA value.
4.  The circle is now calibrated; pointing to other objects involves rotating the telescope until the
circle reads the target’s RA.
Example:  Suppose you want the setting circles calibrated.  This is done by pointing the telescope
at a bright reference star of known coordinates, such as Vega (RA 18h 37m, Dec +38
◦
47
′
).  The
observer centers Vega in the eyepiece, then rotates the RA circle until it reads Vega’s RA (18h
37m), and adjusts the Dec circle to read Vega’s Dec (+38
◦
47
′
).
(b)  Declination Setting Circle
The Declination setting circle is mounted on the declination axis.  It is marked in degrees from
−90
◦
to +90
◦
, corresponding to the full range of declination coordinates.  The declination circle
is fixed relative to the telescope tube and indicates the current declination pointing.  Unlike the
RA circle, it does not need frequent recalibration once properly zeroed.  The circle can be zeroed
using a star of known declination.
Setting circles provide a manual method for locating celestial objects by coordinates.  First, you need
to calibrate both circles using a known reference star.  Then, for a target at coordinates (α,δ), rotate
8

InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
the telescope in declination until the Dec circle readsδ, and right ascension until the RA circle readsα.
Idea 1: Intuitive understanding
Imagine you’re at an astronomy camp with a manual equatorial mount telescope.  You polar-align
it perfectly, then point it at Vega (RA 18h 36m) at exactly 7:00 PM local time (which is at your
meridian), and you even set the RA setting circle to 18h 36m.  Then you just...  leave it there, no
tracking, no touching.  You come back at 3:00 AM to show your friend Vega, but it’s totally not
in the eyepiece anymore, and you only see some random patch of sky.  Your friend asks, “Hey,
what RA does the setting circle show right now, and why isn’t Vega there?” Explain what went
wrong and calculate roughly how many hours of RA the circle is off by now, assuming Sideral
Time = Solar Time.
Answer:
Earth is rotating.  So even though they set the circle to Vega’s RA at 7 PM, the mount isn’t
moving, so the sky basically slides past the telescope.  Vega drifted west out of the field, minutes
after they left.
When they come back at 3 AM, that’s 8 hours later.  The setting circle is still stuck at 18h 36m
because they never moved the scope, but the sky’s meridian has moved forward in RA by about
8 hours (since 1 solar hour≈1 sidereal hour roughly).  So the circle is literally 8 hours behind ,
it shows 18h 36m, but the meridian is now at like 2h 36m RA (next day).  That’s why Vega isn’t
there; it set hours ago, and they’re pointing at some random RA from earlier.  So, if they don’t
track, their RA setting circle becomes useless after a short time.  You again have to recalibrate it
again as mentioned above.
Example 1: Point to a star
Consider the star Antares, which has approximate coordinates:
Right Ascension (RA) = 16h 29m 24s,Declination (Dec) =−26
◦
25
′
55
′′
To point at Antares, the observer rotates the telescope in declination until the Dec circle reads
approximately−26
◦
26
′
.  Then, while keeping the declination fixed, the observer rotates the
telescope along the RA axis until the RA circle reads 16h 29m 24s.  The telescope should now be
pointed at Antares.
