4.  Manual Handling of Equatorial Mounts
Equatorial mounts include manual controls for both coarse and fine positioning.  These controls allow
the observer to point the telescope without motorized assistance.
(a)  Locking Knobs and Free Movement
Each axis of an equatorial mount is equipped with a locking knob or clutch mechanism (check the
figure on page 7).  When these knobs are loosened, the telescope can be moved freely by hand
9

InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
along that axis.  This allows for rapid, large-angle movements.  For example, to move the telescope
from Right Ascension 6h to 14h, the observer would:
i.  Loosen the RA locking knob.
ii.Physically (with free hand) rotate the telescope tube around the RA axis until it is approxi-
mately at the desired position.
iii.  Tighten the RA locking knob to secure the position.
The same procedure applies to the declination axis:  loosening the Dec knob allows the telescope
to be tilted up or down freely.  Once the target is roughly centered in the finderscope, the knob is
tightened to prevent further movement
(b)  Fine Adjustment Cables (Slow-Motion Controls)
After locking the axes approximately on target, fine adjustment is done using slow-motion control
cables (check the figure on page 7).  These are flexible cables connected to gears on each axis.
Turning the cable’s knob rotates the gear, moving the telescope slowly.
The RA slow-motion control rotates the telescope in right ascension at a rate much slower than
manual turning.  One full turn of the knob might move the telescope only a few arcminutes in RA.
The Dec slow-motion control provides similar fine movement in declination.  It is used to center
objects vertically in the field of view.  Unlike the RA control, it does not need to be continuously
turned during tracking, unless correcting for polar alignment errors.
Tip 1: Handling in IOAA 2025 (My Personal Experience)
So in IOAA 2025, the telescope exam was indoors, we had to point the scope at a screen showing
a star field and match it to RA and Dec coordinates we’d calculated earlier.  The catch was that
the equatorial mount’s RA axis was locked, so you could only move it using the fine adjustment
knob on the polar axis.  The Dec axis, though, was completely free,you could just tilt the tube
by hand.
Took me a minute to figure that out,  ngl.  I wasted like two minutes trying to use the fine
adjustment for declination because that’s what we practiced with in camp, and I thought that
knob was basically detached or broken in the exam setup.  Well, I was panicking so much that it
eventually ruined that part of the test (OT 01.3).  Once I realized it, I just moved the declination
axis with freehand (without the knob).  So, in the olympiad test, always check which axes are
actually drivable before you start.  Don’t assume all knobs work.  Sometimes you just gotta
freehand it.
Also, don’t forget to remove the cover from the telescope aperture and the eyepiece :D (From
Trishav’s (Nepal’s IAO and IOAA delegate) and Teodor’s (Romanian IOAA, IOAA Jr & IAO
Medalist) IAO 2023 experience).
You can check the question here.  and here are the things we saw on the screen.
5.  Dovetail mount
Alright, so about dovetails,they’re basically the metal rails that connect your telescope tube to the
mount head.  They slide into a clamp on the mount and get locked in with one or two screws.  Super
simple, but important.  (In the photo below, Lovemandy and Vixen are the names of the producers,
not a specific type of dovetail).  And also, check the figure on page 7, that reflector has its own tube
ring, and you can slide that in the dovetail.  Check any of these YouTube video from here.
10

InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
Tip 2: Accidental slipup in IOAA
In the heat of an olympiad round under time pressure and all, it’s easy to confuse the dovetail
locking screw with the RA/Dec locking knobs.
•The dovetail lock just holds the telescope on the mount.  If you loosen it mid-task, your whole
scope can slide out or rotate.  Tighten it once at the start and leave it alone.
•
The RA and Dec locks control whether you can move the telescope around each axis.  Loosen
them to move freely, tighten to hold position.
If you accidentally loosen the dovetail instead of an axis lock, your tube might swing or droop,
and you’ll waste time re-balancing.  Before you even touch the fine-adjustment knobs check if
the dovetail is secure or not (Give the tube a gentle wiggle).  Also check, which axis locks are
actually working?  (RA locked?  Dec free?  Vice versa?)
6.  Latitude Adjustment
The latitude adjustment on an equatorial mount sets the angle of the polar axis to match your observing
location’s latitude.  Basically, you tilt the mount so it points at the celestial pole,Polaris in the north,
Sigma Octantis in the south.
There’s usually a scale on the mount side with degrees marked.  You set it to your latitude (like 27
◦
for Kathmandu, 40
◦
for New York).  Some mounts have a locking bolt; others use a fine-adjust screw.
Once set, you don’t need to touch it unless you move to a different location.
Tip 3: Latitude adjustment
Say in IOAA, you’re asked to point to a certain (α,δ) (like in IOAA 2015).  If you don’t set the
latitude right, polar alignment will be off from the start and eventually will drift in Declination,
even if you try to align manually.  In timed tasks, you might waste minutes trying to fix drift
instead of doing the actual pointing.
Check the latitude scale before you start.  If it’s way off, fix it first.  During indoor rounds, they
usually pre-set it, but verify anyway.  If the mount has a fine-adjust for latitude, use it for final
tweaking after rough polar alignment.
7.  Counterweight System in Equatorial Mounts
The counterweight is a weighted mass mounted on a shaft opposite the telescope on German equatorial
11

InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
mounts.  Its purpose is to balance the telescope’s weight around the polar (RA) axis.  (check the figure
on page 7)
The counterweight shaft extends from the opposite side of the polar axis from the telescope.  The
counterweight itself slides along this shaft and can be locked at different positions.  The basic principle
is torque balance:
W
