Declination axis, adjust the telescope’s position in its mounting rings (tu until it remains balanced at
any Dec angle.  It’s just an iterative process.
1.2.2    Polar Alignment
Polar alignment means setting your mount’s polar axis to point exactly at the celestial pole (North or
South).  This aligns the mount’s rotation axis with Earth’s, so you only need to turn one knob (RA)
to track stars at a constant sidereal rate, because the declination axis stays fixed relative to the star’s
position.  Without accurate polar alignment, objects will drift out of view even if you’re tracking, and the
drift will be in both RA and Dec directions, making long-exposure astrophotography impossible.  Once
aligned, setting circles becomes accurate, and manual tracking with slow-motion controls works smoothly
along a single axis.  In IOAA 2017 & 2019, the telescope had come with polar alignment by defauly.  You
didn’t have to care for that :D
Setup:
First, set the mount’s latitude scale to your location’s latitude.  Then point the polar axis roughly north
(south in Southern Hemisphere).  Use a compass, but remember magnetic declination,true north isn’t the
same as magnetic north.  You could use Stellarium to locate Polaris (Not applicable for IOAA tho).  And
at night, you can just eyeball it toward Polaris if you’re in the north.
Polar Alignment Using Polaris (Northern Hemisphere):
This method uses Polaris (αUrsae Minoris) to align the equatorial mount’s polar axis with the celestial
north pole.  Polaris is not exactly at the pole; it orbits around it at a radius of approximately 0.75
◦
(45
′
).
For IOAA, if the test is outdoor, then this shall work as they won’t ask for long exposure astrophotography
12

InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
stuff.  But, in real life, this error would eventually pileup causing an error in declination.  Watch this
YouTube video here.  You can you Polarscope method too, which is very accurate.  Watch this YouTube
video to see how a polarscope is used here.
Position Angle (Precise positioning of the Celestial Pole)
If you don’t have a polar scope, you can still accurately align using Polaris by knowing its position angle.
It is the direction from the true celestial north pole to Polaris, measured east from north.  Think of it like
a clock face centered on the true pole, with Polaris moving around it every 24 hours.
Polaris orbits the true pole at a fixed distance of about 0.75
◦
(45
′
).  The
position angle (PA) tells you where along that circle Polaris is at any
given time.  It’s measured in degrees.
•  0
◦
= Polaris directly above the pole (north)
•  90
◦
= Polaris to the right of the pole (east)
•  180
◦
= directly below (south)
•  270
◦
= to the left (west)
For any star (α,δ), Local sideral time being (LST) and observer being
