Now, you need to mentally picture the relationship between the true celestial north pole and Polaris.  The
position angle (PA) tells you exactly where Polaris sits around the pole at that moment.  Now you need
to adjust the mount’s polar axis so that Polaris is positioned at this calculated offset from where the axis
is pointing.
1.3  Dobsonian Mount (Altazimuth)
The Dobsonian mount is a specialized altazimuth mounting system designed
for Newtonian reflectors.  It’s characterized by its simplicity, stability, and low
cost per inch of aperture.  It was developed by John Dobson in the 1960s, and it
revolutionized amateur astronomy by making large-aperture telescopes accessible.
The mount consists of two main components:  a circular ground board typically
made from plywood or similar material, and a rocker box.  The telescope tube
is pivoted in the rocker box.  Watch this YouTube video from here.
Why Use it?
So, for everyday amateur astronomy, a Dobsonian is basically the GOAT for
beginners.  It’s basically a super stable alt-az mount made of cheap materials
like plywood and Teflon, so you get a huge aperture for way less money than an
13

InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
EQ mount.  It’s stupid easy to use,just point and look,without the annoying setup and counterweights of
an EQ mount.  You get significantly more light-gathering power for your buck than a small, wobbly alt-az
mount or an expensive, complicated EQ mount in the same price range.
1.3.1    Design
1. Azimuth Bearing
The azimuth bearing consists of a flat, circular ground board.  Three Teflon pads are mounted on this
board in an equilateral triangle configuration.  The rocker box rests on these pads, with its bottom
surface made from a smooth, low-friction material.  The azimuth bearing allows the entire telescope to
rotate horizontally (360
◦
).
2. Altitude Bearing
The altitude bearing consists of large, circular bearing surfaces attached to the sides of the telescope
tube.  These bear against pads mounted on the sides of the rocker box.  The altitude bearing allows
the tube to tilt vertically.
3.  Manual Movement
Tracking an object requires manual movement in both axes simultaneously.  As Earth rotates, the
observer must gently push the telescope to follow the object’s motion.  This is done by applying light
pressure to the tube.  The bearing surface is smooth, and it allows precise, small movements.  There are
no gears, motors, or slow-motion controls (fine adjustment cables) in a basic Dobsonian.  So, tracking
is entirely manual using your hands.  The required tracking rates depend on the object’s altitude and
14

InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
azimuth, which is the same as the altaz mount.
 ̇
A = ω(sinφ + cosφ cosA tana)
 ̇a = ω cosφ sinA
where
 ̇
A
is azimuth rate and ̇ais altitude rate for an object at azimuthA, altitudea, latitudeφ, and
sidereal rate ω.
4.  Balance
For balance, the telescope tube must be positioned so its center of gravity aligns with the altitude
bearing axis.  If the tube is too front-heavy, it will tend to tilt downward.  If too back-heavy, it will tilt
upward.  Balance is adjusted by sliding the tube forward or backward in the rocker box, or by adding
small counterweights.
Task 3: IOAA 2023
The Telescope test was indoors in IOAA 2023.  They used a Dobsonian inside a Planetarium.  You
had 30 minutes to do actual observations of simulated screens showing an asteroid occultation
and Starlink satellites, then another 30 to write everything up.  For the asteroid, you had to time
exactly when it blocked a star and for how long.  With the satellites, you measured how fast they
moved across the sky and calculated how far apart they were in space.  Then there was a planet
image where you had to identify five of its moons, and finally, you had to find and measure a new
”supernova” in a galaxy image.  Check the problems from here
1.4  German Equatorial Mount (GEM)
German equatorial mount (GEM) is basically the classic EQ mount
you’ve probably seen pictures of.  It has the same two axes as any
equatorial mount:  the polar axis (RA) and the declination axis
(Dec).  The polar axis points at the celestial pole, and you track
by just rotating that axis at the sidereal rate.  The Dec axis lets
you move north/south in the sky.  The working mechanism is the
same as we talked in 1.2 Basic Equatorial Mount (EQ), IT’S NO
DIFFERENT!  In  this  section,  I’ll  talk  about  the  specifics  of  a
GEM.
You’ll mostly find a GEM in IOAA. What makes the GEM special
is its T-shaped design.  The telescope sits on one end of the Dec
axis, and a counterweight sits on the other end to balance it.  The
whole thing rotates around the polar axis, which is attached to a
tripod or pier.  So yeah, the basic idea is exactly the same as other
EQ mounts, but the mechanical layout is different,unlike a fork
mount, where the scope sits between two arms, or a yoke mount, where it’s inside a ring.
1.4.1    The Meridian Flip
So here’s the deal with the meridian flip.  Imagine you’re tracking an object as it rises in the east and
moves west.  On a GEM, the telescope starts off on the east side of the mount.  As the object crosses the
15

InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
meridian (that imaginary north-south line through the zenith), the telescope eventually hits the tripod or
the mount itself,it literally can’t go further without crashing.
To keep tracking, you have to do a “meridian flip.” You stop tracking, quickly slew the telescope to the
other side of the mount (the west side), and restart tracking.  The whole thing takes like 30 seconds if
you’re fast, but during that time, the target is obviously not being tracked.
Why does this matter?  If you’re doing visual observing, it’s just a minor annoyance to you.  You have to
recenter the object after the flip.  But if you’re doing astrophotography and you’re in the middle of a long
exposure, you have to stop the exposure, do the flip, and restart a new exposure.  It breaks your imaging
run.  Watch this YouTube video here.
Tip 4: You never know - IOAA 2017 OT
In IOAA 2017, the participants were asked to use both the Equatorial Mount Telescope in (N1) and
a Dobsonian in (N2).  So, it’s better to learn fundamental of all even before the Telescope camp.
You can just mess and play around there :D
Check the IOAA 2017 Telescope round problems from here.
Task 4: IOAA 2013 - OT2
Check the question from here.  This was EVIL!!  They didn’t allow the participants to look at the
setting circle.  So, practice pointing the objects in the sky without the help of setting circles too :(
1.5  Fork Mount
Fork mount is basically the other common type of equatorial mount you’ll see.  Instead of that T-shaped
German equatorial setup, the fork mount looks like,well, a fork.  Two arms come up from a base and hold
the telescope tube between them.  The whole fork rotates left and right for RA, and the telescope pivots
up and down between the arms for Declination.
If you just put a fork mount on a flat tripod, it’s actually an altazimuth mount.  The base rotates left/right
(azimuth) and the scope tilts up/down (altitude).  To make it equatorial, you have to put it on a wedge.
The wedge is a metal plate that tilts the entire fork so that the base’s rotation axis points at the celestial
pole.  Once you add the wedge and polar align, boom!  it’s an equatorial mount.  If you forget the wedge
or set it to the wrong latitude, your tracking will drift in Dec just like any misaligned EQ mount.  So
16

InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
yeah, it’s literally an alt-az mount tilted sideways.  Once it’s tilted and polar aligned, it tracks just like
any other EQ mount, thus you only need to move the RA axis at the sidereal rate.
Fork mounts are super common on Schmidt-Cassegrains and Maksutovs.  What’s cool is that there’s no
counterweight needed, because the scope is centered between the arms.  That makes the whole thing more
compact and stable.  The tube doesn’t stick out to one side like on a GEM, so it’s less likely to wobble.
But they can get heavy and aren’t as easy to balance if you add a bunch of accessories to the back.
Tip 5: Fine Adjustment on Fork Mounts
Fine adjustment knobs on fork equatorial mounts can feel a bit weird if you’re used to German
equatorials.  On a fork mount, the fine adjustment for RA is usually on the base, near where the
fork rotates, and the Dec fine adjustment is on one of the arms.  The trick is:  always check which
axis you’re actually moving before you start turning.  Sometimes the knobs are close together, and
in the stress of the round, it’s easy to crank the Dec knob thinking you’re tracking in RA, and then
you watch your target slowly drift vertically out of frame.
There aren’t many videos in YouTube on Fork mount.  Well, there’s one, and that’s how to balance it
(here).  You don’t have to worry much about it as Fork Mount isn’t quite asked in IOAA.
1.6  Horseshoe Mount
The horseshoe mount is the big-dad version of a fork mount that you see on huge professional telescopes
like the Hale Telescope at Palomar.  It’s called a horseshoe because instead of a full fork becuase it
has a massive, curved, horseshoe-shaped bearing that holds the telescope at the top.  The horseshoe
handles  all  the  weight  of  the  telescope,  so  it’s  super  stable.   Also,  these  mounts  are  almost  always
computer-controlled because they’re on research scopes, but in principle, you could move them manually
with motors disengaged, but it would just be really slow.
17

InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
2  Accessories
Okay, so you’ve got the telescope and the mount figured out.  But honestly, the real headaches during an
olympiad often come from the accessories.  These are all the little extra pieces you attach to your scope,
like the finderscope, eyepieces, focuser, Barlow lens, and filters, etc.  They’re not just add-ons; they’re
what actually let you see stuff, find stuff, and tweak what you’re seeing.  In IOAA, you’ll be tested on
how well you know these parts,how to use them quickly, how to choose the right one for the job, and how
to fix things when they go wrong mid-task.
2.1  Finderscope
The finderscope is literally your telescope’s GPS,it’s that little mini-telescope or red-dot thing mounted
on top of your main tube.  Its whole job is to help you find stuff without having to squint through the
main eyepiece at a tiny patch of sky.  The main telescope has a narrow field of view, like looking through
a straw, so pointing it blindly at the sky is pretty much hopeless.  The finder gives you a much wider
view, so you can star-hop to your target.  There are two main types of finderscopes:
An Optical FinderA Reflex Finder
1.  Optical Finders
Optical finders are basically mini telescopes mounted on top of your main scope.  They usually come
in sizes like 6x30, 8x50, or 9x60,where the first number is the magnification and the second is the
aperture in millimeters.  A 6x30 has 6x magnification and a 30mm front lens; an 8x50 is brighter and
shows more detail because of the bigger aperture.  They have crosshairs (reticles) inside that you align
with your main telescope’s view.
Because they magnify, optical finders let you see fainter stars than you can with your naked eye.  This
makes star-hopping way easier.  The downside is that they invert the image (usually upside-down and
mirrored), which can be confusing if you’re trying to match what you see to a star chart.  Also, at high
magnification, the field of view is smaller, and if your finder isn’t well aligned, you’ll miss the target
completely.
2.  Reflex Finder
Reflex finders don’t magnify anything.  They have a clear glass window and project a lit reticle (usually
18

InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
a red dot or a circle) that seems to float in the sky.  You look through it with both eyes open, so you
see the dot overlaid on the actual night sky.  They’re super intuitive because there’s no image inversion,
and they have a huge field of view, like around 10 degrees or more, so pointing is fast.
But because there’s no magnification, you can’t see fainter stars through them.  If you’re looking for
something dim, you have to aim using bright stars you can already see with your naked eye.  Parallax
can be an issue too,if your eye isn’t centered, the dot appears to shift relative to the stars.
2.1.1    Aligning the Finderscope
Aligning the finder is step one,  and if you skip it,  you’re gonna have a bad time.  So,  aligning your
finderscope is non-negotiable.  If it’s off, you’ll point the main telescope at the empty sky every time.
Here’s how to do it right, whether you’re in daylight or already at night.  Here’s the step to aligning the
finderscope with the eyepiece:
•  Choose a distant target
You want to do this during the day using a fixed land object.  At night the things in the sky are moving
which makes it more difficult to get the alignment correct.  During the day, set your telescope so that
you can see a distant object, at least 1/4 mile away and farther is better.  That gives a very well defined
target right where the cross arm meets the pole.  But the top of a chimney, a letter on a distant sign,
or something similar can work as well.  This is your alignment target.
•  Center the target in the main telescope
Put in a low- or medium-power eyepiece (like a 25mm) and point the telescope at your target.  Get it
perfectly centered in the eyepiece.  Use the telescope’s slow-motion controls if available, or gently nudge
the tube.  If it’s a star, defocus it slightly so it looks like a big blob,this makes centering way easier.
•  Look through the finder & Adjust
Without moving the main telescope, check the finder.  You’ll likely see the target way off-center, maybe
not even in the field of view.
Finders have either three or six small screws around the mounting bracket.  Usually, three locking
screws (to hold it tight) and three adjustment screws (to push the finder around).  Loosen the locking
screws just enough so the finder can move, but not so much that it flops around.  Then gently turn the
adjustment screws to move the reticle toward the target.  Move it until the target is exactly on the
crosshairs.
•  Recheck and fine-tune
Go back to the main eyepiece.  Is the target still centered?  If not, recenter it in the telescope first,
then check the finder again and tweak further.  This might take a couple of back-and-forths.  Once it’s
aligned, tighten the locking screws evenly.  To be sure, point at another object in a different part of the
sky.  Center it in the main scope and verify it’s also centered in the finder.
Tip 6: Finderscope in an IOAA Round
In an IOAA round, you might not get time to align the finder from scratch.  So, they often give you
a telescope that’s already roughly aligned.  You should never just assume it’s perfect.  Take literally
30 seconds at the start to verify:  point the main scope at the brightest star you can see, center it in
a medium-power eyepiece, and then check the finder.  If the star is dead-center on the crosshairs,
great.  If it’s even a little off, adjust it right then.  Those few seconds will save you from chasing
19

InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
nothing later.
When the task is to find something faint like a galaxy, nebula, or a tight double star, you can’t just
swing the finder around hoping to stumble on it.  Open your star chart (they usually provide one
like in the IOAA 2019, 2025) and identify a bright, easy-to-find star near your target.  Point the
finder at that star first.  Then, using the pattern of fainter stars between that bright star and your
target, move slowly, matching what you see in the finder to the chart.
Task 5: IOAA 2019 – Finderscope Alignment Breakdown
Check the Task 1 from here
Okay, so this is literally the first thing they hit you with in the 2019 telescope round.  They give you
a 150/750 Newtonian (that’s 150mm aperture, 750mm focal length).  You’ve got three eyepieces
(25mm, 10mm) and a 2x Barlow, but for this task, you must use the 25mm.  The telescope is
already polar-aligned, so you don’t need to touch the mount, just the finder.  The task says:  “The
finderscope is NOT aligned at the beginning.  Point the telescope at Saturn and align the finderscope
parallel to the main tube.” They give you 5 minutes.  Sounds simple, but here’s what’s really going
on.
First, you need to actually find Saturn in the main telescope using only the main tube, because the
finder is off.  Since the mount is polar-aligned and tracking, you can’t just swing it around freely.
You’ll have to use the slow-motion controls (probably the RA and Dec fine-adjust knobs) to sweep
the sky until Saturn appears in your 25mm eyepiece.  Saturn is bright and distinctive (rings!), so
once you see it, center it carefully.  Then, without moving the main scope, you look through the
finder.  Saturn will probably be nowhere near the crosshairs.  You now loosen the finder’s adjustment
screws and move the finder until Saturn is centered exactly on the crosshairs.  Tighten everything
down, recheck in the main scope, and if it’s still centered, you’re done.
But here’s the catch:  They say,  “If the alignment of Saturn is not within the crosshair of the
finderscope, the telescope assistant will correct it,and you receive only partial or no points.” That
means if you mess up and hand it over unaligned, they’ll fix it for you, but you lose points.  So
precision matters.
2.2  Eyepiece
Eyepieces are the part you actually look through, they’re like the lens of your telescope, but removable so
you can swap them out for different magnifications.  Think of them as different camera lenses:  wide-angle,
telephoto, etc.  They take the focused light cone from your telescope and magnify it into the image you
see.
Every eyepiece has a focal length, measured in millimeters.  That’s the number printed on the side,like
25mm, 10mm, 6mm, etc.  Shorter focal length = higher magnification.  The magnification formula is super
simple:
Magnification =
Telescope Focal Length
Eyepiece Focal Length
