Task 6: IOAA 2025 – Telescope Task (Eyepiece thing) Breakdown
Check OT01.3 from here
Alright, so this is a classic indoor screen-based telescope task from IOAA 2025.  Your job is to find
object ‘Z’ in that projected sky using your telescope, center it in the eyepiece’s crosshair, and show
it to the examiner.
•  Map Study First
Before you reach your station, you would be provided 20-30 minutes of the question reading time
and would be asked to solve the above part of the question.  You already have the printed map
(Map-OT01).  Mark object ‘Z’ clearly on it and memorize the star pattern around it.  The screen
projection is different in scale from the real sky, so you need to mentally match the pattern, not
the size.
•  Start with High Focal length (Low Power) Eyepiece
Use the 25mm eyepiece first.  It gives a wider field of view, so it’s easier to find the general area
and recognize the star pattern from your map.  Once you see the pattern on the screen through
your scope, slowly move until ‘Z’ is somewhere in the field.  Don’t worry about centering yet.
Just get it in view.
•  Switch to Low Focal Length (High Power) with Crosshair
Then, swap to the 10mm eyepiece (which has a crosshair).  This gives higher magnification and
makes centering precise.  Now,  using fine-adjustment knobs,  nudge the telescope until ‘Z’ is
exactly on the intersection of the crosshair.
•  Scoring Breakdown
The scoring was super specific:
If you use the 10mm eyepiece:
- 2 points for correct eyepiece choice.
- 3 points if the object is anywhere in the field of view.
- 6 points if any part of the object is touching the origin (center) of the crosshair.
If you only use the 25mm eyepiece:
- You only get points if the object is in the field.  No crosshair centering points.
21

InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
In this type of observational task, try until the last second to find the object.  And, until the final
second, even if you fail to find the object with a low-power (25mm) eyepiece, just replace that with
the high-power (10mm) eyepiece.  You could get an extra 2 points just by switching the eyepieces.
So, the key is to use the 25mm for finding and the 10mm for measuring.
2.2.1    Eyepiece Internal Ray Diagram
Starting from the left side, where it says barrel, that’s basically the ”front door” of the eyepiece.  As
the light enters from your telescope, it hits the entrance focal plane.  This is the exact spot where the
telescope’s objective lens or mirror has already done the hard work of creating a tiny, inverted version of
whatever star or planet you’re looking at.  Right there, you’ve got the field stop, which is one of the most
important parts.  It’s essentially a physical ”crop tool”, a ring that blocks out the messy, distorted light
at the edges so you get a crisp, circular view instead of a blurry mess around the rim.  It defines your
field angle α, which is basically how much of the sky you’re seeing at once.
Once the light passes that entrance window, it hits the glass.  The first glass is the field lens.  Its main job
is to catch the diverging light rays and bend them back toward the center so they don’t just get lost inside
the mount.  Then, those rays pass through the eye lens, which is the one you actually look into.  This lens
collimates (a fancy way of saying it makes the rays parallel again), so your eye can relax and focus on
them as if they were coming from infinitely far away.  The space between these lenses, held steady by that
spacer ring, is what determines the overall focal length and how much magnification you’re getting.
Finally, we get to the part where you actually put your face:  the eye relief and exit pupil.  The exit pupil
is that tiny, concentrated circle of light on the far right where all those rays cross over.  If you want to see
the full apparent field of view, your eye’s pupil has to be hovering exactly at that spot.  The distance you
have to hold your eye back from the lens to see clearly is the eye relief.  If that distance is too short, you’re
basically smashing your eyelashes against the glass just to see the whole image; if it’s long, it’s a much
more chill experience.  It’s all about finding that perfect ”sweet spot” where the light being projected
matches up perfectly with your own eye.
Let’s talk about some important terms in the next page.
22

InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
2.2.2    Field Stop Diameter
Alright, so you know how every eyepiece has a certain field of view?  The thing that actually determines
that is the field stop.  It’s a physical ring or diaphragm inside the eyepiece that cuts off the light cone.
Think of it like a window inside the eyepiece barrel:  only light that passes through this window gets to
your eye.  Anything outside it is blocked.
