So if you have a 150mm telescope and you’re using a 25mm eyepiece at 30× magnification:
Exit Pupil =
150
30
= 5 mm
Why does this matter?  Your eye’s pupil only opens about 7mm max in total darkness for a young person,
less if there’s light pollution or you’re older.  If the exit pupil is bigger than your eye’s pupil, you’re
wasting light.  The extra light just hits your iris.  If it’s too small (like below 1mm), the view gets dim
and starts showing floaters in your eye.  For deep-sky viewing, you want an exit pupil around 2–7mm.  For
planets, you can go smaller (1–2mm) because you’re looking for detail, not brightness.
2.2.4    Eye Relief
Eye relief is the distance between the eyepiece lens and your eye where you can still see the whole field
of view.  It’s measured in millimeters.  Long eye relief (15–20mm) means you can hold your eye back
comfortably, maybe even with glasses on.  Short eye relief (less than 10mm) means you have to press your
eyeball almost against the glass.
If you wear glasses (especially if you have astigmatism), you need long eye relief so your glasses don’t
scratch the lens and you can still see the full field.  Even if you don’t wear glasses, short eye relief is
annoying.  Your eyelashes smear the lens, you get kidney-beaning (where part of the field blacks out if
you move slightly), and it’s just uncomfortable for long sessions.
Some good eyepieces have adjustable eyecups that roll up or down to help you position your eye just
right.  In an olympiad setting, you usually can’t choose your eyepiece, but if you have options, pick one
23

InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
with comfortable eye relief so you’re not squinting and losing time.
