Task 7: IOAA 2018 Telescope Round – Problems 2
Check the problem from here.  The telescope had focal lengthf
o
= 700mm and the focal length of
the eyepiece was f
e
= 26mm.
This one was purely observational.  They displayed text on a screen somewhere in the room, and
you had to point the telescope at it, focus, and read what it said.  The text listed:  two planets
(Saturn, Jupiter), two Messier objects (M15, M74), and one equatorial coordinate (+30° 21h).  You
wrote these down for points,8 points each.
But the catch is that you couldn’t just look with your eyes.  You had to use the telescope.  That
means:
•First, you had to find the screen in your finderscope, and also, they tested mount handling:  could
you smoothly point the scope from one part of the room to another?  If it were an equatorial
mount, you might need to loosen clutches and move it manually since you’re not tracking the sky.
•  Then, center it in the main telescope using a low-power eyepiece (probably the 26mm).
•  Possibly switch to a higher-power eyepiece if the text was small.
Idea 2: Calculating FOV of a telescope - IOAA 2022 Telescope round (Problem 2)
Check the problem from here.  So, in this problem you have to calculate the TFOV of the telescope.
But this time you dont have any other information.  YOU JUST HAVE YOUR TELESCOPE
TUBE, ALT-AZ MOUNT and YOUR EYEPIECE. Let’s learn how to calculate the TFOV with
this setup only.
Let’s start with the definition of TFOV itself.  It’s basically how much of the sky (or the ground)
24

InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
you can see at once through the eyepiece.  The steps you do it is:
1.  Pick the reference object
Point your telescope at something easy to recognize here on Earth, like a building with clear
features (windows, edges, etc.).  You want something that won’t move, so you can measure
accurately.
2.  Move the Azimuth Knob
Look through your telescope and pick a starting point on the building (maybe the left edge of a
window).  Now, without moving the telescope up or down, just turn it slowly sideways (using
one axis, like the azimuth slow motion knob) until that starting point moves out of view and
another landmark appears at the opposite side of your view.  Before doing this, note the initial
azimuth reading in the dial (α
1
)
3.  Repeat!
Keep doing that.  Count how many entire fields of view (n) you move past until you’ve gone from
one end of the building to the other (or between two clear landmarks).  Let’s say you moved
n full “frames” or fields of view.  After completingnframes, check the dial and note the final
azimuth reading in the dial (a
2
).  Thus ∆α (change in azimuth) is simply (∆α = α
2
− α
1
)
4.  Formulate
The math is super simple
TFOV =
|∆α|
n
So if your telescope moved through 6° total (∆α= 6°) and you counted 5 fields of view (n= 5),
then:
TFOV =
6
5
= 1.2
◦
That means each field of view is 1.2° wide.
This was the exact idea behind IOAA 2022 (OT2).  You chose a reference point, then do the smart
work as above.  And then after this, we had to calculate AFOV similar to IOAA 2018 OT1 (above
discussed Problem 1).
2.3  Barlow Lens
It’s a tube with a lens inside that you put between your telescope and your eyepiece, and it multiplies
your magnification.  If you have a 2x Barlow, it doubles the power of whatever eyepiece you use.  So your
25mm eyepiece suddenly acts like a 12.5mm, and your 10mm acts like a 5mm.  It’s way cheaper than
buying a whole set of short focal length eyepieces.  Watch this YouTube video on Barlow from here.
25

InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
2.3.1    How Barlow Works
A Barlow is a diverging lens.  It spreads out the light cone coming from your telescope before it reaches
the eyepiece, which makes the eyepiece ”think” the telescope has a longer focal length.  The magnification
factor is usually written on the side:  2x, 3x, sometimes 2.5x.  The formula to calculate the effective
magnification simple:
New Effective Magnification (M
ef f ective
) = Original Magnification (M
original
)× Barlow Factor (BF )
Or, in focal length terms:
Effective Focal Length (F
ef f ective
) =
Eyepiece Focal Length (F
original
)
Barlow Factor (BF )
So yeah, a 2x Barlow turns a 25mm eyepiece into a 12.5mm one.  But remember, it also changes the
effective focal ratio of your system.  If your telescope is f/5, adding a 2x Barlow makes it act like f/10 for
that eyepiece.  I’ve covered IOAA 2016 (T7) (Which was on Barlow) in Instrumentation - 1 (Page 32-34).
Check it out!
Task 8: IOAA 2019 - Barlow in IOAA
Check the Task 2 from here.  This is IOAA 2019 Telescope Round Questions.
So in IOAA 2019, Task 2 was all about Saturn.  You had to mark Titan’s position, draw the rings
to scale, and measure Titan’s angular distance and position angle.  They gave you a 10mm eyepiece,
a 25mm, and a 2x Barlow.  The note said:  “For task 2, we recommend using 10 mm eyepiece and
Barlow 2x.” That’s a huge hint!  They want you at high magnification so you can see Titan clearly
and measure the ring dimensions accurately.
Why?  Because Saturn’s rings are only about 43
′′
across.  At 30× (with the 25mm), Titan might
look like a tiny dot close to the planet, and the rings will be small.  But with the 10mm + 2x
Barlow, you’re at 150× (750mm scope / (10mm/2) = 150×). That makes Saturn bigger in the
field, so you can clearly separate Titan from the planet’s glare, see the ring orientation and width
more precisely, and make more accurate measurements for angular distance.  But the catch is, high
magnification also magnifies atmospheric turbulence.  If the seeing is bad, the image might be shaky,
making drawing harder.  Still, in 2019 they recommended it, so they expected decent conditions.
2.4  Focuser
Focus in a telescope is achieved when light rays from a distant object, such as a star or planet, are
converged (brought together) at a single point by the telescope’s lens or mirror.  When this convergence
point coincides perfectly with the focal plane of our eyepiece or camera sensor, the object appears sharp
and clear.  Focusers help with this by sliding the eyepiece back and forth, effectively moving the focal
plane of the eyepiece along with it.
Tip 7: Setup the Focuser early
Before you start your Telescope work in IOAA, adjust your focuser.  If you don’t do it, then you
won’t see anything from the eyepiece.  So, first, start with a reference object.  If you’re indoors
pointing at a screen, use text or a sharp pattern or if you’re outdoor, use a bright star or planet.
26

InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
Turn the focuser all the way in (eyepiece closest to the telescope), then slowly turn it out until the
image snaps into focus.  If you go past it, backtrack slowly.  At high power, the focus point is really
narrow and sensitive, like a millimeter of movement can ruin it.
In tasks where you switch eyepieces, you’ll likely need to refocus each time.  Different eyepieces
have different optical positions, so when you swap, the focus changes.  And if you add a Barlow
lens, you’ll need to focus again, usually moving the focuser outward.
2.5  Filters
Filters are essential tools in observational astronomy that allow you to see specific details by controlling
which colors of light reach your eye or camera.  Think of them like specialized sunglasses for your telescope.
Each filter is designed to highlight certain features while suppressing others.  The filters screw into a
standard eyepiece barrel.  The contrast differences are subtle, and some observers report little or no
observable differences.  It may take patience and experience to recognize the differences.
2.5.1    Broadband Filters
Broadband filters are the most common type of visual filter.  They allow a wide range of wavelengths
to pass through, typically covering a large portion of the visible spectrum.  Their main purposes are to
reduce glare, enhance contrast for specific colors, and protect your eyes from excessively bright objects
like the Moon.
1.  Luminance Filters
A luminance filter, often called a neutral density (ND) filter, reduces the overall brightness of an
image without changing its color balance.  It works like sunglasses for your telescope, cutting down
the intensity of light evenly across the visible spectrum.  These are primarily used for observing very
bright objects.
The most common application is lunar observation (Provided in IOAA 2016).  Without a filter, the full
Moon can be painfully bright through a telescope, causing eye fatigue and reducing the visibility of
subtle surface details.  A typical moon filter transmits only 13%, 18%, or 25% of the incoming light.
The transmission percentage is usually marked on the filter’s rim.  For example, an ND25 filter allows
25% of light to pass, cutting 75% of the brightness.
27

InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
2.  Planetary Color Filters
These are colored glass filters that transmit specific color ranges while blocking others.  They are
identified by Wratten numbers, a standard system developed by Kodak.  Each color enhances certain
planetary features by increasing contrast.
•Red (Wratten #25):  Transmits red and orange light while blocking blue and green.  Useful for
increasing contrast on Martian surface features and dust storms.
•Green (Wratten #58):  Transmits green and yellow-green light.  Excellent for enhancing the cloud
structures and the Great Red Spot on Jupiter, and for observing Venus’s atmospheric features.
•
Blue (Wratten #80A): Transmits blue and violet light.  Helps to see details in the cloud tops of
Venus and Mars.
•Yellow (Wratten #12):  Transmits yellow and some green light.  Useful for improving contrast on
Martian surface features and reducing glare on Venus and Jupiter.
In IOAA, you may be asked to select the appropriate filter to sketch a specific planetary detail or to
estimate the visibility of a lunar feature under filtered conditions.
2.5.2    Narrowband Filters
Jarrowband filters are the next level up from those colored planetary filters.  They’re not just letting
through “red” or “blue” light.  They’re designed to isolate super-specific colors of light that come from
glowing gas in space.  Think of them like a super precise bouncer at a club, only light with one exact
wavelength gets in, everything else gets bounced.
What They Actually Isolate:
Space isn’t just full of white starlight.  Nebulae and certain stars emit light at very specific wavelengths
called emission lines.  This happens when atoms get excited and then drop down energy levels, emitting a
photon with a precise energy (and therefore color).  The most important ones for visual astronomy are:
•Hydrogen-alpha (H-α):  Wavelength = 656.28 nm.  This is deep red light emitted by hydrogen gas
when an electron falls from the 3rd to the2nd energy level.  It’s what makes emission nebulae like the
Orion Nebula glow.
•Oxygen-III (O-III): Wavelengths = 495.9 nm and 500.7 nm.  These are teal-green lines from doubly
ionized oxygen.  They dominate in planetary nebulae (like the Ring Nebula) and supernova remnants.
•Sulfur-II (S-II): Wavelength = 672.4 nm.  This is a deeper red line from ionized sulfur.  It’s often
used in astrophotography to map different gas structures, but it’s less common for visual use.
A narrowband filter has a super-tiny bandpass.  Bandpass is the range of wavelengths it lets through.  A
good visual O-III filter might have a bandpass of 10-12 nm wide.  An ultra-narrow one for photography
can be as tight as 3 nm.  The narrower it is, the more it blocks other light (like light pollution), but also
the dimmer the view gets.
Why You’d Use One:
Let’s say you’re looking at the Orion Nebula (M42) from a suburban backyard.  Without a filter, it looks
like a faint gray smudge because the skyglow from streetlights washes it out.  If you screw in an H-alpha
or O-III filter, the filter blocks most of that white streetlight glow but lets the specific red or teal light
from the nebula through.  Suddenly, the nebula pops out with way more contrast and detail.  It literally
turns an invisible object into a visible one.
28

InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
The Trade-Off:
Narrowband filters make everything darker.  You’re blocking like 90% of the total light.  So, stars will
look much fainter, often disappearing entirely unless they’re emission-line stars themselves.  You’ll need a
decently large telescope (like 6-inch or bigger) to gather enough of that specific light.
Math for Narrowband Filters:
The filter’s transmission is described by its profile T (λ).  The light you see is:
S =
∫︂
F (λ)· T (λ)dλ
whereF(λ) is the spectrum of the object.  You need to know:  if an object is strong in O-III (like the
Ring Nebula), use an O-III filter.  If it’s an H-II region (like Orion), use H-alpha.
2.5.3    Solar Filter
Solar  filters  are  a  special  and  absolutely  critical  category  of  filters.   Their  purpose  is  to  allow  safe
observation of the Sun by blocking more than 99.999% of its incoming light and harmful infrared (IR)
and ultraviolet (UV) radiation. Warning:  Using an improper filter or misusing a solar filter can cause
instant and permanent eye damage, including blindness.  Solar observation requires strict safety protocols.
Solar filters work by reflection and absorption.  They are not like neutral density filters; they are specifically
engineered to reject the immense heat and radiation of the Sun.  There are two main types based on
where they are placed:
•
Aperture Filters (Full-Aperture Filters):  These are placed over the front opening (aperture) of
the telescope.  They block the sunlight before it enters the optical tube.  This is the only safe method
for visual solar observation with most telescopes.
•  Herschel Wedges (for Refractors):  This is a specialized prism-based system used with refracting
telescopes.  It reflects a tiny, safe percentage of sunlight to the eyepiece while diverting the vast majority
of the heat and light out of the system.
In an olympiad setting, solar observation tasks are rare due to the safety risk and scheduling, but they
can occur.  If presented, it will be under tightly controlled conditions.
2.5.4    Light Pollution Filters
Light pollution filters are designed to block the specific wavelengths emitted by common artificial light
sources, particularly mercury vapor (at 546 nm) and high/low-pressure sodium vapor lamps (around
589 nm and 570-620 nm), while allowing the continuum and emission lines from celestial objects to pass
through.
2.6  Stopwatch
In IOAA practical rounds, a stopwatch is often provided to make timing-based measurements.  Here, we’ll
see different types of questions that have appeared in IOAA and Stopwatch.  In the circular, they’ll say
whether you’ll be provided with a stopwatch, headlight, or neither (3-4 months ahead of the IOAA itself).
So with that in mind, you can guess what kind of question might be asked.  I’ve covered some of the
problems that appeared with the stopwatch.
29

InstrumentationOlympiad Handout
Idea 3: IOAA 2016 – Drift Time Calculation
Check IOAA 2016 OT3 from here.  In this task, you’re given a telescope, a moon filter, an eyepiece
with a crosshair, and a stopwatch.  Your objective is to estimate the diameter of Mare Serenitatis (a
large, circular lunar mare) as a fraction of the Moon’s overall diameter using the drift-time method.
Setup:
First, you point the telescope at the Moon and attach the moon filter.  This filter reduces the
Moon’s intense brightness, making it comfortable to view and preventing eye fatigue during the
detailed measurement.  The eyepiece has a crosshair, which is essential for precise timing as objects
drift across the field of view due to Earth’s rotation.
The method of calculation is based on a simple principle:  the time it takes for an object to drift
across the fixed crosshair in the eyepiece is proportional to its angular size.  Since the telescope’s
field of view is constant, a larger angular object takes longer to drift past the crosshair.
Measurement Steps:
•Align the Crosshair:  Orient the crosshair so that the Moon drifts perpendicularly across one
of the lines.  Typically, you align it east-west, as the Moon’s drift is primarily in right ascension.
(Checks your ability to point, focus, and use an eyepiece with a crosshair.)
•
Time the Full Moon’s Diameter:  Place the Moon’s eastern limb on the crosshair, then start
the stopwatch the moment the limb touches the line.  Stop the watch the moment the western
limb leaves the crosshair.  Record this time as t
Moon
.
•
Time Mare Serenitatis:  Now center Mare Serenitatis in the field.  Similarly, record how long it
takes for this circular mare to drift completely across the same crosshair.  Record this as t
MSr
.
Calculation:
The ratio of the drift times gives the ratio of their angular diameters:
D
MSr
D
Moon
=
t
MSr
t
Moon
Therefore, the estimated diameter of Mare Serenitatis as a fraction of the lunar diameter is simply
the ratio of the two measured times.
